Fix colour sampling of images in the test device

Carried locally against mupdf 1.28.2. Not submitted upstream.

Two defects in the image sampling paths of fz_test_fill_compressed_8bpc_image()
and fz_test_fill_other_image() made colour detection read uninitialised memory,
so the same binary over the same file returned different Color= results between
runs. For ProFile Counter that meant a page could be classified black-and-white
or colour at random.

1. The colour converter is declared as

       void (*convert)(fz_context *, fz_color_converter *, const float *src, float *dst)

   and every other caller in the library passes (src, dst). Both call sites here
   pass (dst, src): the uninitialised ds[] is handed over as the source, the
   converted result is written into cs[] (which held the real pixel data), and
   the code then tests ds[], which is still uninitialised stack memory. Pass the
   arrays in the declared order.

2. fz_test_fill_other_image() computes the per-pixel component count as
   pix->n - 1, unconditionally assuming an alpha channel. For a pixmap without
   alpha the count becomes 0, so the sampling loop never reads the pixmap and
   leaves cs[] unwritten. Subtract the alpha channel only when there is one, and
   bail out rather than sample if a pixmap reports no colour components at all.

Defect 1 is the one that affects ordinary ICC and Flate encoded images and is a
regression against the 1.6 series this project previously used, which was
deterministic on the same files.

To refresh after a mupdf version bump: apply by hand, adjust, then regenerate
with `git diff -- source/fitz/test-device.c` from within the submodule.

diff --git a/source/fitz/test-device.c b/source/fitz/test-device.c
index 7a82d34c5..9bc4c0d7b 100644
--- a/source/fitz/test-device.c
+++ b/source/fitz/test-device.c
@@ -229,7 +229,7 @@ static void fz_test_fill_compressed_8bpc_image(fz_context *ctx, fz_test_device *
 				for (k = 0; k < n; k++)
 					cs[k] = fz_read_byte(ctx, stream) / 255.0f;
 
-				cc.convert(ctx, &cc, ds, cs);
+				cc.convert(ctx, &cc, cs, ds);
 
 				if (is_rgb_color(dev->threshold, ds[0], ds[1], ds[2]))
 				{
@@ -284,7 +284,17 @@ fz_test_fill_other_image(fz_context *ctx, fz_test_device *dev, fz_pixmap *pix, f
 	else
 	{
 		fz_color_converter cc;
-		unsigned int n = (unsigned int)pix->n-1;
+		unsigned int n = (unsigned int)pix->n - sa;
+
+		/* A pixmap without an alpha channel has no component to subtract, and
+		 * the sampling loop below reads exactly n components per pixel. If n
